`Bishop` and `Knight` share the same interface but have structurally different logic — sliding along a line vs jumping to fixed offsets.

```java
class Bishop extends Piece {
    List<Move> getCandidateMoves(Board board) {
        List<Move> moves = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int[] dir : DIAGONAL_DIRECTIONS) {
            moves.addAll(slideInDirection(board, dir));
        }
        return moves;
    }
}

class Knight extends Piece {
    List<Move> getCandidateMoves(Board board) {
        List<Move> moves = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int[] offset : KNIGHT_OFFSETS) {
            addIfOnBoardAndNotOwnPiece(board, offset, moves);
        }
        return moves;
    }
}
```

`slideInDirection` walks squares one at a time until it hits a piece or the board edge; the knight's loop is a fixed, small set of offsets with no sliding at all — same method signature, genuinely different algorithms underneath.

`isInCheck` scans every opposing piece's attack squares against the king's square; `isCheckmate` tries every legal move to see if any escapes check.

```java
boolean isInCheck(Color color) {
    Square kingSquare = board.findKing(color);
    for (Piece opponentPiece : board.getPieces(opposite(color))) {
        if (opponentPiece.getCandidateMoves(board).stream()
                .anyMatch(m -> m.getTo().equals(kingSquare))) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

boolean isCheckmate(Color color) {
    if (!isInCheck(color)) return false;
    for (Piece piece : board.getPieces(color)) {
        for (Move move : moveValidator.getLegalMoves(piece, board)) {
            if (!wouldLeaveKingInCheck(move, color)) return false; // an escape exists
        }
    }
    return true; // in check, and nothing removes it
}
```

`getLegalMoves` already filters out self-check moves, so `isCheckmate` only needs to confirm the legal-move set is non-empty *and* that check persists — it reuses `isInCheck` rather than duplicating attack-scanning logic.

`CastlingMove.execute(board)` moves both the king and the rook and marks both as having-moved, in one method the board just calls.

```java
class CastlingMove extends Move {
    void execute(Board board) {
        board.movePieceRaw(king.getSquare(), kingDestination);
        board.movePieceRaw(rook.getSquare(), rookDestination);
        king.setHasMoved(true);
        rook.setHasMoved(true);
    }

    void undo(Board board) {
        board.movePieceRaw(kingDestination, king.getSquare());
        board.movePieceRaw(rookDestination, rook.getSquare());
        king.setHasMoved(false);
        rook.setHasMoved(false);
    }
}
```

`Game.applyMove(move)` calls `move.execute(board)` without an if/else for castling versus a normal move — the polymorphism from §11 means the caller never branches on move type at all.
